If _j_=1, \(Mc^{(0)}_{2r+z}\) and \(Ms^{(0)}_{3r+p}\), _p_=0, 1 are solutions of the first kind, proportional to \(C_{2r+z}\) and \(Se_{2r+p}\), respectively.

Thus

**20.6.15**

\[\begin{array}{l}{\cal C}e_{2r}(z,q)\!=\!\frac{ce_{2r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}?q \right)ce_{2r}(0,\,q)}{(-1)^{r}A^{2r}_{2}}\,Mc_{r}^{(0)}(z,q)\\ \\ {\cal C}e_{2r+1}(z,q)\!=\!\frac{ce^{\prime}_{2r+1}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}?q\right) ee_{2r+1}(0,\,q)}{(-1)^{r+1}\sqrt{q}Ai^{r+1}}\,Me_{2r+1}^{(0)}(z,q)\\ \\ Se_{2r}(z,q)\!=\!\frac{se^{\prime}_{2r}\left(0,\,q\right)se^{\prime}_{2r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2},\,q\right)}{(-1)^{r}qB^{2r}_{2}}\,Ms_{2r}^{(0)}(z,q)\\ \\ Se_{2r+1}(z,q)\!=\!\frac{se^{\prime}_{2r+1}(0,\,q)se_{2r+1}\left(\frac{\pi}{2},\,q\right)}{(-1)^{r}\sqrt{q}Bi^{r+1}}\,Ms_{2r+1}^{(0)}(z,q)\\ \end{array}\]

The Mathieu-Hankel functions are

**20.6.16**

\[\begin{array}{l}M^{(0)}_{r}(z,q)\!=\!M^{(t)}_{r}(z,q)\!+\!iM^{(0)}_{r}(z,q)\\ M^{(t0)}_{r}(z,q)\!=\!M^{(t)}_{r}(z,q)\!-\!iM^{(t)}_{r}(z,q)\\ M^{(t)}_{r}\!=\!Me^{(t)}_{r}\!\mbox{or}\,Ms^{(t)}_{r}.\end{array}\]

From **20.6.7-20.6.11** and the known properties of Bessel functions one obtains

**20.6.17**

\[\begin{array}{l}M^{(0)}_{2r+z}(z\!+\!in\!\pi,\,q)\\ \hskip 85.358268pt=\!(-1)^{*n}\![M^{(0)}_{2r+z}(z,q)\!+\!2\! in\!M^{(0)}_{2r+z}(z,q)]\\ M^{(0)}_{2r+z}(z\!+\!in\!\pi,\,q)\\ \hskip 85.358268pt=\!(-1)^{*n}\![M^{(0)}_{2r+z}(z,q)\!-\!2\! nM^{(0)}_{2r+z}(z,q)]\\ M^{(t0)}_{2r+z}(z\!+\!in\!\pi,\,q)\\ \hskip 85.358268pt=\!(-1)^{*n}\![M^{(t0)}_{2r+z}(z,q)\!+\!2\! nM^{(0)}_{2r+z}(z,q)]\\ \end{array}\]

where _M_=_Mc_ or _Ma_ throughout any of the above equations.

